Here is the whole program. It is the coding agent's `Program.cs`, as the Getting started page shows it. Its one call starts a terminal host with the catalogue it names.

```csharp
using LouisAgent.Core.Web;
using LouisAgent.Core.Anthropic;
using LouisAgent.Core.Ollama;
using LouisAgent.Core.OpenAi;
using LouisAgent.Core.Telemetry;
using LouisAgent.Core.Hosting;
using LouisAgent.Cli;
using LouisAgent.Coding;

// Usage: LouisAgent.CodingCli [prompt]. It loads what this demo's catalogue names.
await CliHost.RunAsync(args, CodingCatalog.Create(), new HostServices(WebAccess.Create, OtlpTelemetry.Instance, Providers: [AnthropicProvider.Instance, OllamaProvider.Instance, OpenAiCompatibleProvider.Instance]));
```

The settings go in `.env.secrets`, beside a `.env` in the program's `config/` folder. These are the two the page names:

```text
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-key-here
LLM_PROFILE=ollama
```

`CodingCatalog` is the coding demo's own catalogue. To make an agent of your own, write your own catalogue that names your identities and packs, and pass it in its place. The page's `Program.cs` is otherwise the whole program. The `LouisAgent.Coding` using line goes, and your own namespace takes its place.

The CLI reads its settings from the `config/` folder you name, or the nearest one it finds. Run it from inside that folder. The `CliHost` call and the host's behaviour are in the CLI project doc.

